      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $q$ plus $3.$\n\nII. $2$ is greater than $x.$\n\nIII. $a$ is a divisor of $b.$",
      "worked_solution": "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $q$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(a,b)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
