    "q-175366": {
      "dom_id": "step-q175366",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $P(y):$ ${y}^{2}-1=8$\n\nII. $Q(a,b):$ ${a}^{2}+{b}^{2}$\n\nIII. $R(x,y):$ $\\frac{x}{y}\\in N\\cap Q$",
      "worked_solution": "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(a,b)$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
