    "q-175387": {
      "dom_id": "step-q175387",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Consider the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x,y):$ $x+y<3,$\n\nwhere the universal set is ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded regions represents the truth set of the predicate?",
      "worked_solution": "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U={R}^{2}.$\n\nWe need to find $(x,y)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $x+y<3$\n\nNow, we convert the inequality into the slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & <3 \\\\ y & <-x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's sketch the truth set $T$ of $P(x,y):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253115/assets/bde3d6ee1e58d33088ffc49913e1cd8a299f0ea91cdf4024fd0b63a598ebf456.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
